mprove the bounds on the number of Tesler matrices.\n\n### March 5, 2018\n\n### 11:00 AM\n\n### AP&M 7321\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the poset and asymptotics of Tesler Matrices (https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.00866)\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.8 years ago; Title: On the poset and asymptotics of Tesler Matrices ... In 2014, Drew Armstrong defined a poset on such matrices and conjectured that the characteristic polynomial of this poset is a power of $(q-1)$.\nTitle: On the poset and asymptotics of Tesler Matrices\nAuthors: Jason O'Neill\nDate: Thu Feb  2 23:13:33 2017\n\nTesler matrices are certain integral matrices counted by the Kostant partition function and have appeared recently in Haglund's study of diagonal harmonics. In 2014, Drew Armstrong defined a poset on such matrices and conjectured that the characteristic polynomial of this poset is a power of $(q-1)$. We use a method of Hallam and Sagan to prove a stronger version of this conjecture for posets of a certain class of generalized Tesler matrices. We also study bounds for the number of Tesler matrices and how they compare to the number of parking functions, the dimension of the space of diagonal harmonics.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn Equitable List Arboricity of Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.08926)\nciteturn0academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.2 years ago; Equitable list arboricity, introduced by Zhang in 2016, generalizes the notion of equitable list coloring by requiring the subgraph induced by each color class to be acyclic (instead of edgeless) in addition to the usual upper bound on the size of each color class. ... Zhang conjectured that any graph $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable for each $k$ satisfying $k \\geq \\lceil (1+\\Delta(G))/2 \\rceil$. We verify this conjecture for powers of cycles by applying a new lemma which is a general tool for extending partial equitable, arborable list colorings.\nTitle: On Equitable List Arboricity of Graphs\nAuthors: Hemanshu Kaul, Jeffrey A. Mudrock, Michael J. Pelsmajer\nDate: Wed Aug 19 14:56:33 2020\n\nEquitable list arboricity, introduced by Zhang in 2016, generalizes the notion of equitable list coloring by requiring the subgraph induced by each color class to be acyclic (instead of edgeless) in addition to the usual upper bound on the size of each color class. Graph $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable if an equitable, arborable list coloring of $G$ exists for every list assignment for $G$ that associates with each vertex in $G$ a list of $k$ available colors. Zhang conjectured that any graph $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable for each $k$ satisfying $k \\geq \\lceil (1+\\Delta(G))/2 \\rceil$. We verify this conjecture for powers of cycles by applying a new lemma which is a general tool for extending partial equitable, arborable list colorings. We also propose a stronger version of Zhang's Conjecture for certain connected graphs: any connected graph $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable for each $k$ satisfying $k \\geq \\lceil \\Delta(G)/2 \\rceil$ provided $G$ is neither a cycle nor a complete graph of odd order. We verify this stronger version of Zhang's Conjecture for powers of paths, 2-degenerate graphs, and certain other graphs. We also show that if $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable it does not necessarily follow that $G$ is equitably $(k+1)$-list arborable which addresses a question of Drgas-Burchardt, Furmanczyk, and Sidorowicz (2018).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLinear Arboricity Conjecture (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/LinearArboricityConjecture.html)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Linear Arboricity Conjecture ... Image: la(G)<=[(Delta(G)+1)/2],   | (1) ... Equivalently, its edges can be partitioned into at most this many linear forests, each a forest whose connected components are path graphs. ... They also defined topological linear arboricity Image: la_(top)(G) by requiring each linear forest, after adjoining the graph ends represented by its one-way infinite graph paths, to contain no subset homeomorphic to a circle in the space formed by adjoining all graph ends to Image: G.\n\n# Linear Arboricity Conjecture\n\nThe linear arboricity conjecture states that every finite graph Image: G has\n\nImage: la(G)<=[(Delta(G)+1)/2],   | (1)\n\nwhere Image: la(G) is its linear arboricity, Image: Delta(G) is its maximum vertex degree, and Image: [x] is the ceiling function (Akiyama et al. 1981). Equivalently, its edges can be partitioned into at most this many linear forests, each a forest whose connected components are path graphs.\n\nThe elementary lower bound is\n\nImage: [(Delta(G))/2]<=la(G),   | (2)\n\nsince each linear forest has maximum vertex degree at most 2. Thus the conjectured upper bound is either this lower bound or one more. Akiyama et al. (1981) established the conjecture for cubic graphs and 4-regular graphs, and Alon (1988) proved an asymptotic form.\n\nAurichi et al. (2026) proved that the linear arboricity conjecture for finite graphs is equivalent to the same bound for all infinite graphs of finite maximum vertex degree. They also defined topological linear arboricity Image: la_(top)(G) by requiring each linear forest, after adjoining the graph ends represented by its one-way infinite graph paths, to contain no subset homeomorphic to a circle in the space formed by adjoining all graph ends to Image: G. For finite maximum vertex degree, Aurichi et al. (2026) proved\n\nImage: la(G)<=la_(top)(G)<=la(G)+1.   | (3)\n\nEvery Image: 2k-regular graph of girth at least Image: 2k also satisfies Image: la_(top)(G)<=k+1 (Aurichi et al. 2026).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn Equitable List Arboricity of Graphs · ProbXiv (https://probxiv.com/problems/on-equitable-list-arboricity-of-graphs-